WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.500
Myoglobin draws a hyperbola.

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.420
Hemoglobin draws an S. Where does the S come from?

3
00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.010
Hemoglobin has four sites.

4
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.810
And the whole molecule can take either of two overall shapes.

5
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:20.973
T, for tense, is held by extra salt bridges between the subunits.

6
00:00:21.973 --> 00:00:23.693
Each heme in T holds oxygen weakly.

7
00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:27.497
R, for relaxed, has fewer of those bridges.

8
00:00:28.457 --> 00:00:30.177
Each heme in R holds oxygen tightly.

9
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:36.030
In one illustrative fit, a T site is half full only near 110 torr.

10
00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:38.890
An R site is half full below 1 torr.

11
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.040
A molecule does not wait for oxygen to change shape.

12
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.220
Even empty, it flickers between T and R.

13
00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:49.783
But with no oxygen, T is far more stable.

14
00:00:50.723 --> 00:00:52.343
R molecules are almost absent.

15
00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:54.787
So a sample is a mix.

16
00:00:55.647 --> 00:00:59.987
Its fullness is the average of each shape's fullness, weighted by how much of each

17
00:00:59.987 --> 00:01:00.987
is present.

18
00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:06.647
At 20 torr, about a fifth of the molecules are R. Together the mix is about

19
00:01:06.647 --> 00:01:07.647
one-third full.

20
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:14.110
At 100 torr, nearly all the molecules are R. The mix is about 98% % full.

21
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.160
Between those two points, about two-thirds is delivered.

22
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.080
But notice what changed.

23
00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.760
The fraction of R climbed with oxygen.

24
00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:30.683
Why? R holds oxygen far better than T. So each oxygen bound makes R a little more

25
00:01:30.683 --> 00:01:31.683
favorable.

26
00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:35.377
Oxygen is a reactant that only R uses well.

27
00:01:36.337 --> 00:01:38.897
Adding oxygen pulls the balance from T toward R.

28
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.650
Follow one molecule as it fills.

29
00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:46.390
Each oxygen multiplies the odds of R by the same large factor.

30
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.300
That factor is how much more tightly R holds oxygen than T does.

31
00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:57.783
At low pressure, nearly everything is T. The curve follows T's weak curve and barely

32
00:01:57.783 --> 00:01:58.783
rises.

33
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:04.427
Through the middle, each oxygen tips more molecules into R. Their empty sites fill

34
00:02:04.427 --> 00:02:06.767
easily. This is the steep part.

35
00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.840
At high pressure, nearly everything is R. The curve flattens onto R's curve near

36
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:13.840
full.

37
00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:18.250
Predict. Suppose T and R held oxygen equally well.

38
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.330
What shape would the curve have?

39
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:27.950
A plain hyperbola.

40
00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:32.070
Oxygen would favor neither shape, so the mix would never shift.

41
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:38.520
So cooperativity is oxygen shifting the mix toward the shape that binds oxygen best.

42
00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.820
The four sites share one shape, so a shift changes all four at once.

43
00:02:43.783 --> 00:02:47.183
The Hill coefficient scores how steep the S is at half full.

44
00:02:48.083 --> 00:02:50.203
Identical, independent sites score 1.

45
00:02:51.163 --> 00:02:53.023
Hemoglobin scores about 2.8.

46
00:02:53.967 --> 00:02:56.467
The score can be no larger than the number of sites.

47
00:02:57.387 --> 00:02:58.407
It measures steepness.

48
00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:00.367
It does not count anything.

49
00:03:01.417 --> 00:03:06.817
Predict. A carrier as steep as hemoglobin, but with myoglobin's P50.

50
00:03:07.777 --> 00:03:08.777
How much would it deliver?

51
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:20.640
Almost none. Its steep part sits far below 20 torr, so it is still full in the

52
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.640
muscle.

53
00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:26.090
A hyperbola with hemoglobin's P50 delivers only about a third.

54
00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:30.410
Hemoglobin needs both: an S, and an S in the right place.

55
00:03:31.383 --> 00:03:36.283
The S comes from two shapes that differ in grip, and oxygen shifting the mix between

56
00:03:36.283 --> 00:03:36.503
them.
